.TH IOSTAT 1M
.SH NAME
iostat \- report I/O statistics
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iostat
[
.B \-t
] [
.B \-i
]
[ interval [ count ] ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Iostat
delves into the system and reports certain statistics kept about
input-output activity.
For typewriters collectively, the number of input and output characters
are counted.
Also, on each clock tick,
a tally is made in
one of four categories, depending on whether the system is
executing in user mode, in `nice' (background)
user mode, in system mode, or idle.
.PP
The optional
.I interval
argument causes
.I iostat
to report once each
.I interval
seconds.
The first report is for all time since a reboot and each
subsequent report is for the last interval only.
.PP
The optional
.I count
argument restricts the number of reports.
With an
.I interval
and no
.IR count ,
.I iostat
reports until it is stopped.
With neither, it prints one report and exits.
.PP
With no option argument
.I iostat
gives the percentage of time the system has
spent in each of the four categories mentioned above.
.PP
The following options are available:
.TP
.B \-t
Report the number of characters of terminal IO per second as well.
.TP
.B \-i
Report the percentage of time spent in each
of the four categories mentioned above, one per line and named.
.SH FILES
None.
.I Iostat
reads
.BR dk_time ,
.B tk_nin
and
.B tk_nout
through
.IR kctl (2)
and opens no device.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
kctl(2), pstat(1M), ps(1)
.SH BUGS
.PP
.B Note:
.B there are no disk columns.
v7's
.I iostat
reported, for each of three disks, the transfers per minute and the
milliseconds per seek and per transfer, and crossed those with the four
CPU states to make the 32-way tally it reads.
On this system
.B dk_busy
is written by no driver, so the I/O half of every subscript is permanently
zero and only four of the 32 slots ever move;
.BR dk_busy ,
.B dk_numb
and
.B dk_wds
are deliberately not exported at all, an exported variable that nobody
updates being worse than an absent one.
The columns return with task 38 of
.BR kernel/TODO.md .
There is no
.B cp_time
on this system and none is wanted.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's
.B \-s
(the 32 raw slots) and
.B \-b
(buffer-cache statistics, out of an
.B io_info
structure this kernel does not have) are gone with them, as are the
undocumented
.B \-d
and
.BR \-a .
.PP
.B Note:
the clock runs at
.B HZ
ticks per second, which is 250 here and was 60 on the PDP-11.
Nothing in the report is expressed in ticks, so this shows up only in
the resolution of the percentages.
.PP
Things can change while
.I iostat
is running.
